Patch 0002. Delivers the repository audit that patch 0001 was written without, and
builds the capability the audit says is worth building.

AUDIT (docs/06_AUDIT_FINDINGS.md) — the headline finding inverts the brief:

- ThermalForge-Liquid-Cooling holds NOTHING reusable. One commit, 19 .tsx, 2,206
  lines of marketing site and portal shell. No calculations, no models, no Python,
  no tests. The eight-item extraction list in docs/03 §6 is void; that section is
  marked superseded. Every thermal capability in gridforge/thermal is original work.
- GridOS is the real asset: 99 Python modules, ~13,100 lines, 55 commits, 23 test
  modules, CI, MIT licensed, and unusually honest about its own scope.
- DERIM is not accessible under this account; its port stays empty.
- Nothing in any repository is E4 or above. Nothing is field-validated. Nothing is
  deployed. That is the accurate position and it is now written down.

ACCURACY-CLAIM CORRECTION (audit finding A1):

- app/page.tsx and the FAQ in lib/site.ts described the reference architectures as
  "validated engineering designs". Validated against what? No test data, no
  commissioned installation, no field measurement. "Validated" is E4; these designs
  are E1. Corrected to say what is true: modelled and internally reviewed, not
  validated against a delivered project. This was the highest-exposure claim on the
  site and it sat against the repository's own stated policy.
- Finding A2 left open: neither GridForge-AI nor ThermalForge has a LICENSE file.

BEHIND-THE-METER SUPPLY (gridforge/power/btm.py):

- Firm capacity is not nameplate. PV contributes zero to an envelope that must hold
  at 03:00; a duration-limited battery contributes only what it sustains over the
  ride-through window; a gas engine contributes nameplate less forced outage.
  Getting this wrong optimistically is how a study gets stopped at the utility desk.
- The grid constraint's relief is now on-site firm supply where options are declared,
  and stays an honest no-op where they are not - we do not model capacity that cannot
  be bought in a metro with no connections available this decade.
- Options are ordered by lead time before cost: in this market weeks are scarcer
  than euros.

TIME TO POWER (gridforge/envelope/time_to_power.py):

- Capacity without a date is not a decision. Each relief carries a lead time and
  reliefs run in parallel, so the energisation curve falls out of the ladder for
  free, and one item sets the date. On the reference hall that item is the
  transformer at 160 weeks, not any of the construction work.
- New report section 3 carries the curve, the item that sets the date, and the
  comparison across architectures.

GRIDOS INTEGRATION (gridforge/integrations/gridos/adapter.py):

- Wires the value-stacked dispatch and ROI backtest behind the EnergyValueModel port,
  plus firm-capacity-from-duration which needs no GridOS import at all.
- GridOS is imported lazily, inside the call: gridforge keeps its zero-dependency
  guarantee, a study that asks no economics question never touches it, and its
  absence raises NotWired rather than returning a silent zero. MIT, attributed.
- Forecasting, MPC, telemetry, digital twin and protocol adapters stay unwired until
  a paid workflow needs them.

RECOMMENDATION OBJECTIVE IS NOW EXPLICIT (max compute / min cost per rack / fastest
to power). A study that hides a judgement call inside a scoring formula is hiding it
from the buyer.

REFERENCE PROJECT now shows the number only this engine produces: the same synthetic
hall carries 28 racks on its grid connection alone and 62 racks with behind-the-meter
firm supply, with every step costed and the schedule driver named.

43 tests pass, up from 28. Still no third-party dependency at import time - the
architecture test now permits a guarded lazy import inside an adapter and nowhere else.

